        "id": 91305,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-15T01:25:44.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I would imagine that about the same level of technology (measuirng the position and speed of individual air molecules and processing all that data fast enough to be relevant) would enable both firing every bullet exactly on-target and flawlessly predicting the outcome of a dice throw.&nbsp; In either case, I consider the chaos-theory mathematicians who call such measurements impossible to be quitters.</p><p>True randomness is impossible in a clockwork cosmos.&nbsp; That's part of why I find the idea so seductive in fiction.</p>",
