      {
        "id": 91306,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-15T02:54:52.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I would imagine that about the same level of technology (measuirng the position and speed of individual air molecules and processing all that data fast enough to be relevant) would enable both firing every bullet exactly on-target and flawlessly predicting the outcome of a dice throw.&nbsp; In either case, I consider the chaos-theory mathematicians who call such measurements impossible to be quitters.True randomness is impossible in a clockwork cosmos.&nbsp; That's part of why I find the idea so seductive in fiction.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I'm not familiar with the term \"clockwork cosmos\". &nbsp;Would you be so kind as to expound upon your meaning?</p>",
