      {
        "id": 91308,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-15T03:13:37.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>jsz2 wrote:</b><br><blockquote>I'm not familiar with the term \"clockwork cosmos\". &nbsp;Would you be so kind as to expound upon your meaning?\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\nIt means that all reality operates with the precision of an incredibly vast and cosmic machine, with every atom a \"gear\" in this vast \"clock\", so that nothing is ever truly random, but rather the deterministic effect of a hugely long and complex chain of cause and effect.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Ah.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I've often wondered about that, actually. &nbsp;Most randomness is like that of course, but I've wondered if there is true randomness at a quantum level. &nbsp;I can't really see how we would find out, though, besides taking two identical universes and running them to see if they diverged (which seems rather impractical to me). &nbsp;Or figuring out how all the quantum stuff actually works.</p>",
